a)	Create and run a Spring Boot application using Spring Initializr.
Aim: To create and run a Spring Boot application using Spring Initializr.

Procedure:
1.	Go to https://start.spring.io/
2.	Select:
o	Project: Maven
o	Language: Java
o	Spring Boot: Latest Version
3.	Add Dependency:
o	Spring Web
4.	Click Generate
5.	Extract and open in VS Code / IntelliJ
6.	Run the main application.
Project Structure:
src/main/java/com/example/demo
-- DemoApplication.java
--HelloController.java

Main Class:
package com.example.demo;
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
@SpringBootApplication
public class DemoApplication
{
public static void main(String[] args)
{
SpringApplication.run(DemoApplication.class, args);
}
}
Controller Class:
package com.example.demo;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;
@RestController
public class HelloController {
@GetMapping("/")
public String home() {
return "Welcome to Spring Boot Application";
}
@GetMapping("/hello")
public String hello() {
return "Hello World from Spring Boot!";
}
}

Run the Application:
Run the main class → DemoApplication.java

Output (Browser):
URL 1:
http://localhost:8080/
Output:
Welcome to Spring Boot Application

URL 2:
http://localhost:8080/hello
Output:
Hello World from Spring Boot!

	
b)	Configure the project structure and dependencies.
Aim:To configure the project structure and dependencies of a Spring Boot application created using Spring Initializr.

Project Structure:
After generating the project, the folder structure will look like:
demo/
 ├── src/
 │   ├── main/
 │   │   ├── java/com/example/demo/
 │   │   │   ├── DemoApplication.java
 │   │   │   ├── controller/
 │   │   │   │   └── HelloController.java
 │   │   │   ├── service/
 │   │   │   ├── repository/
 │   │   │   └── model/
 │   │   └── resources/
 │   │       ├── application.properties
 │   │       └── static/
 │   └── test/
 │       └── java/com/example/demo/
 ├── pom.xml
 └── mvnw

Explanation of Structure:
•	DemoApplication.java → Main class (entry point)
•	controller/ → Handles HTTP requests
•	service/ → Business logic layer
•	repository/ → Database interaction
•	model/ → Entity classes
•	application.properties → Configuration file
•	pom.xml → Dependency management
Dependencies Configuration (pom.xml):
Add required dependencies in pom.xml:
<dependencies>

    <!-- Spring Boot Starter Web -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Spring Boot Starter Data JPA -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- H2 Database (for testing) -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>com.h2database</groupId>
        <artifactId>h2</artifactId>
        <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Lombok (optional) -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.projectlombok</groupId>
        <artifactId>lombok</artifactId>
        <optional>true</optional>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Spring Boot Test -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
        <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

</dependencies>


Application Properties Configuration:
server.port=8080

spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:testdb
spring.datasource.driverClassName=org.h2.Driver
spring.datasource.username=sa
spring.datasource.password=

spring.jpa.database-platform=org.hibernate.dialect.H2Dialect
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update
spring.h2.console.enabled=true

Sample Controller to Test Configuration:
@RestController
public class HelloController {

    @GetMapping("/test")
    public String test() {
        return "Project Configured Successfully";
    }
}


Run the Application:
Run DemoApplication.java

Output (Browser):
http://localhost:8080/test
Output:
Project Configured Successfully


